ered by x=2). Actually x=2 covers (2,2),(2,3),(2,4). So column 2 points are covered. Column 3: (3,2),(3,3) (x=3 covers (3,2),(3,3)). Column 4: (4,2) (not covered). Column 5: (5,1) covered by y=1. So uncovered: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5),(4,2). That's 5 points left, but we have only 2 sunny lines left. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so at most 2 points per line (since columns 1 and 4). So total capacity = 4 points, insufficient for 5 points. So fails.\n\nThus k=2 seems impossible for n=5 as well.\n\nThus the argument that k=2 is impossible seems plausible, though the reasoning in the solution may be flawed in details but the conclusion holds.\n\nNow we need to check if k≥4 is impossible for all n≥3. The solution's argument is flawed, but maybe the conclusion is still correct. Let's try to find a construction for k=4 for some n.\n\nWe need to find n lines, exactly 4 sunny, n-4 non-sunny, covering all points.\n\nWe can try to use n-4 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4, and use 4 sunny lines to cover columns n-3, n-2, n-1, n. Let's test for n=7 (so we have 7 lines total, 4 sunny, 3 non-sunny). Use vertical lines for columns 1,2,3 (non-sunny). Then we need to cover columns 4 (size 4), 5 (size 3), 6 (size 2), 7 (size 1) with 4 sunny lines. Let's see if possible.\n\nColumns:\n\n- Column 4: points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4)\n- Column 5: (5,1),(5,2),(5,3)\n- Column 6: (6,1),(6,2)\n- Column 7: (7,1)\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So across columns 4-7 (4 columns), each sunny line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity = 4*4 = 16 points. The total number of points in columns 4-7 is 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. So capacity is enough.\n\nNow we need to assign points to lines such that each line is collinear.\n\nWe can try to assign each line to cover one point from each column (i.e., 4 points per line). That would be ideal.\n\nWe need to find 4 lines that each intersect columns 4-7 at distinct points, covering all points.\n\nWe can think of constructing lines with slopes that produce a pattern.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slope -1/3, -1/2, -1, etc. But slope -1 is not allowed. So we need slopes not -1, 0, infinite.\n\nWe can try to use lines that go through (4,1) and (5,2) and (6,3) and (7,4) but (7,4) not in T_n (since 7+4=11 > n+1=8). So not.\n\nAlternatively, we can use lines that go through (4,4) and (5,3) and (6,2) and (7,1). That's line x+y=8, slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we cannot use the anti-diagonal line.\n\nWe need to find lines that cover points across columns but not slope -1.\n\nPerhaps we can use lines of slope -1/2: For example, line through (4,4) and (6,3) slope = (3-4)/(6-4) = -1/2, sunny. It also passes through (5,3.5) not integer. So covers (4,4) and (6,3). But (6,3) not in T_n (since column 6 has only points (6,1),(6,2)). So not.\n\nLet's try to systematically search for a covering for n=7, k=4.\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines to cover 10 points. Each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So we need to assign points to lines such that each line gets at most one point per column.\n\nWe can try to assign each line to cover exactly one point from each column, which would give 4*4 = 16 capacity, but we only need to cover 10 points, so some lines may have fewer points.\n\nWe need to ensure collinearity.\n\nWe can treat this as a problem of finding 4 lines that collectively cover all points in columns 4-7.\n\nWe can try to use the following approach: Use lines that each cover a point in column 7 (only one point (7,1)). Since we have 4 lines, we can assign (7,1) to one of them, and the other three lines will not have a point in column 7.\n\nSimilarly, column 6 has points (6,1),(6,2). We need to assign each to a different line (since each line can have at most one point per column). So we need at least 2 lines covering column 6 points.\n\nColumn 5 has 3 points, need at least 3 lines covering them.\n\nColumn 4 has 4 points, need at least 4 lines covering them.\n\nThus we need at least 4 lines (which we have) to cover column 4 points, each line covering one of them. So each line must have a point from column 4. So each line will have a point from column 4.\n\nThus we can assign each line to a distinct point in column 4: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4). Then we need to assign column 5 points (5,1),(5,2),(5,3) to three of the lines (distinct). Column 6 points (6,1),(6,2) to two lines. Column 7 point (7,1) to one line.\n\nThus each line will have a point from column 4, and possibly also a point from column 5,6,7.\n\nNow we need to find lines that pass through the assigned points.\n\nThus we need to find a matching of points such that for each line i, the points assigned (col4_i, maybe col5_i, col6_i, col7_i) are collinear.\n\nWe can try to assign:\n\n- Line A: (4,1) and (5,2) and (6,3?) not in T_n, (7,4?) not in T_n. So line A would cover (4,1) and (5,2). Slope = (2-1)/(5-4) = 1. So line A: y = x - 3. At x=6, y=3 (not in T_n). At x=7, y=4 (not in T_n). So line A covers (4,1),(5,2). Good.\n\n- Line B: (4,2) and (5,3) slope = (3-2)/(5-4) = 1, same slope. So line B: y = x - 2. At x=6, y=4 (not in T_n). At x=7, y=5 (not in T_n). So line B covers (4,2),(5,3). Good.\n\n- Line C: (4,3) and (6,1) maybe? Let's compute slope: (1-3)/(6-4) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot pair (4,3) with (6,1). Pair (4,3) with (6,2): slope = (2-3)/(6-4) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. So line C: passes through (4,3) and (6,2). Equation: slope -0.5, y-3 = -0.5(x-4) => y = -0.5x +5. So at x=5, y = -2.5 +5 = 2.5, not integer. At x=7, y = -3.5 +5 = 1.5, not integer. So line C covers (4,3),(6,2). Good.\n\n- Line D: (4,4) and (7,1) maybe? Slope = (1-4)/(7-4) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So cannot pair (4,4) with (7,1). Pair (4,4) with (5,1) slope = (1-4)/(5-4) = -3, sunny. So line D: passes through (4,4) and (5,1). Equation: y-4 = -3(x-4) => y = -3x + 16. At x=6, y = -18+16 = -2, not in T_n. At x=7, y = -21+16 = -5. So line D covers (4,4),(5,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered all points in column 4 (4 points) and column 5 (points (5,1),(5,2),(5,3) covered by lines D, A, B respectively). Column 6 points: (6,1) not covered yet, (6,2) covered by line C. Column 7 point (7,1) not covered.\n\nWe have used all 4 lines, but we still have uncovered (6,1) and (7,1). We cannot add more lines. So we need to adjust assignments to include (6,1) and (7,1) in the existing lines.\n\nWe could try to assign (6,1) to line D perhaps, but line D currently passes through (4,4) and (5,1). Let's compute slope between (4,4) and (6,1): slope = (1-4)/(6-4) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. So line through (4,4) and (6,1) would be slope -1.5. Does it also pass through (5,? )? At x=5, y = 4 + (-1.5)*(5-4) = 4 - 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So line would cover (4,4) and (6,1). Then we could assign (5,1) to a different line. Let's try to reassign.\n\nGoal: cover (6,1) and (7,1) as well.\n\nWe could try to assign (7,1) to line A maybe? Line A currently passes through (4,1) and (5,2). Let's compute slope = 1. At x=7, y = (7-3) = 4, not 1. So not.\n\nLine B passes through (4,2) and (5,3), slope 1, at x=7, y = 5, not 1.\n\nLine C passes through (4,3) and (6,2), slope -0.5, at x=7, y = -0.5*7 +5 = 1.5, not 1.\n\nLine D passes through (4,4) and (5,1), slope -3, at x=7, y = -3*7 + 16 = -5, not 1.\n\nThus none cover (7,1). So we need to adjust assignments to include (7,1). Perhaps we can assign (7,1) to a line anchored at column 4 point (4,1) with slope (1-1)/(7-4) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nThus maybe we need to use a non-sunny line for column 7 point (7,1). But we have only 3 non-sunny lines (vertical lines for columns 1-3). Could we use a horizontal line y=1 as a non-sunny line to cover (7,1) and other points? But we already used vertical lines for columns 1-3. We could replace one vertical line with horizontal y=1, but then column 1 point (1,1) would be uncovered. However, we could cover (1,1) with a sunny line anchored at (1,2) maybe? But that would be vertical? Let's think.\n\nAlternatively, we could use a slope -1 line (non-sunny) to cover (7,1) and other points. For example, line x+y=8 passes through (7,1),(6,2),(5,3),(4,4). That's slope -1, non-sunny. That line would cover many points in columns 4-7. Indeed, x+y=8 covers (4,4),(5,3),(6,2),(7,1). That's 4 points. That's a non-sunny line. So we could use that as one of the non-sunny lines. Then we would have 2 remaining non-sunny lines (vertical lines for columns 1-2 maybe) and 4 sunny lines to cover the rest.\n\nBut we have only n-4 = 3 non-sunny lines for n=7. So we could allocate one non-sunny line as the diagonal x+y=8, covering those 4 points. Then we have 2 non-sunny lines left, which we could use as vertical lines for columns 1 and 2 (or maybe horizontal y=1). Then we have 4 sunny lines left to cover the remaining points.\n\nLet's try this approach for n=7, k=4.\n\nNon-sunny lines:\n\n- L_NS1: diagonal x+y=8 (covers (4,4),(5,3),(6,2),(7,1))\n- L_NS2: vertical x=1 (covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5),(1,6),(1,7?) Actually column 1 has points (1,1) to (1,7) but we only have up to (1,7) because n=7, column 1 has points (1,b) for b=1..7. So vertical line x=1 covers all column 1 points.\n- L_NS3: vertical x=2 (covers (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(2,5),(2,6)? Actually column 2 has points (2,b) for b=1..6). So vertical line x=2 covers all column 2 points.\n\nNow we have used 3 non-sunny lines, covering columns 1,2, and diagonal points (4,4),(5,3),(6,2),(7,1). Remaining points to cover are:\n\n- Column 3: points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4) (since column 3 has size n+1-3 = 5? Actually n=7, column 3 has points (3,b) for b=1..5? Let's compute: h_c = n+1-c = 8-c. So column 3 has h_3 = 5 points: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4),(3,5). Wait check: a+b ≤ n+1 = 8. For a=3, b ≤ 5. So yes, (3,1)-(3,5). So column 3 has 5 points.\n\n- Column 4: points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3) (since (4,4) covered by diagonal). So column 4 has points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3).\n\n- Column 5: points (5,1),(5,2) (since (5,3) covered by diagonal). So column 5 has (5,1),(5,2).\n\n- Column 6: point (6,1) (since (6,2) covered by diagonal). So column 6 has (6,1).\n\n- Column 7: no points left (since (7,1) covered by diagonal).\n\nThus remaining points: column 3: (3,1)-(3,5); column 4: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3); column 5: (5,1),(5,2); column 6: (6,1). That's total points: 5 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 11 points.\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines left to cover these 11 points. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, so across columns 3-6 (4 columns), each line can cover at most 4 points. So total capacity = 4*4 = 16 > 11, so possible.\n\nNow we need to assign points to lines.\n\nWe have columns 3,4,5,6. Column 3 has 5 points, need at least 5 lines to cover them if each line can cover at most one point per column. But we have only 4 lines. So we cannot cover all 5 points in column 3 with only 4 lines. However, we could use a non-sunny line to cover some column 3 points, but we have used all non-sunny lines (vertical x=1, x=2, diagonal). So we cannot.\n\nThus this approach fails because column 3 has too many points.\n\nThus maybe we need to allocate non-sunny lines differently: use vertical lines for columns 1 and 3, and use diagonal for columns 4-7, leaving column 2 to be covered by sunny lines. Let's try a different allocation.\n\nWe need to cover all points with 4 sunny lines and 3 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. We need to allocate them to cover columns with many points.\n\nThe columns with many points are the leftmost columns: column 1 has n points, column 2 has n-1 points, column 3 has n-2 points, etc. So we might want to use vertical lines for the leftmost columns to reduce the load on sunny lines.\n\nBut we have only n-4 non-sunny lines. For n=7, n-4 = 3 non-sunny lines. So we can cover at most 3 columns fully with vertical lines. So we can cover columns 1,2,3 with vertical lines, leaving columns 4-7 to be covered by sunny lines. Column 4 has 4 points, column 5 has 3 points, column 6 has 2 points, column 7 has 1 point. That's total 10 points. We have 4 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column, so across 4 columns, each line can cover at most 4 points, total capacity = 16 > 10, so possible.\n\nNow we need to assign points in columns 4-7 to 4 sunny lines, each line anchored at some column 1? Actually we have used vertical lines for columns 1-3, so we have no anchors left. But we can have sunny lines that are not anchored at column 1; they can be any lines. They just need to be distinct and sunny.\n\nWe need to cover points:\n\n- Column 4: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4)\n- Column 5: (5,1),(5,2),(5,3)\n- Column 6: (6,1),(6,2)\n- Column 7: (7,1)\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines. Each line can cover at most one point per column. So we need to assign each column's points to distinct lines.\n\nColumn 4 has 4 points, so each line must cover exactly one of them. So each line will have a point from column 4.\n\nColumn 5 has 3 points, so three of the lines will have a point from column 5, one line will not.\n\nColumn 6 has 2 points, so two lines will have a point from column 6.\n\nColumn 7 has 1 point, so one line will have a point from column 7.\n\nThus we need to assign points to lines such that each line's assigned points are collinear.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines each passing through a distinct point in column 4, and also possibly passing through a point in column 5,6,7 as per assignment.\n\nWe can try to find such lines.\n\nLet's denote the points in column 4 as A1=(4,1), A2=(4,2), A3=(4,3), A4=(4,4). We need to assign each to a line L_i.\n\nNow we need to assign points in column 5: B1=(5,1), B2=(5,2), B3=(5,3). We need to assign each to a line distinct from each other, and not to the line that already has a point from column 5? Actually each line can have at most one point from column 5, so we need to assign each B_j to a distinct line among the 4 lines.\n\nSimilarly for column 6: C1=(6,1), C2=(6,2). Assign each to distinct lines.\n\nColumn 7: D1=(7,1). Assign to one line.\n\nThus we need to find a set of 4 lines L_i such that:\n\n- L_i passes through A_i (distinct column 4 point).\n- For some subset of lines, L_i also passes through a B_j (distinct column 5 point).\n- For some subset, L_i also passes through a C_k (distinct column 6 point).\n- For one line, L_i also passes through D1.\n\nAll points assigned to a line must be collinear.\n\nThus we need to find a matching of points such that for each line, the points assigned are collinear.\n\nWe can try to find lines that pass through (4,1) and (5,2) and (6,3?) not in T_n, (7,4?) not in T_n. So line through (4,1) and (5,2) slope = (2-1)/(5-4) = 1, sunny. It also passes through (6,3) not in T_n, (7,4) not in T_n. So line L1: (4,1)-(5,2) covers A1 and B2.\n\nLine L2: (4,2)-(5,3) slope = (3-2)/(5-4) = 1, sunny. It also passes through (6,4) not in T_n. So L2 covers A2 and B3.\n\nLine L3: (4,3)-(5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-4) = -2, sunny. It also passes through (6,-1) not in T_n. So L3 covers A3 and B1.\n\nNow we have used B1,B2,B3 all covered. We have used A1,A2,A3. We still have A4=(4,4) uncovered. We have column 6 points C1=(6,1), C2=(6,2) and column 7 point D1=(7,1) uncovered.\n\nWe have one line left (L4) to cover A4 and possibly some of C1,C2,D1.\n\nWe need to find a line through A4=(4,4) that also passes through one of C1, C2, D1.\n\nCheck line through (4,4) and (6,2): slope = (2-4)/(6-4) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,4) and (6,1): slope = (1-4)/(6-4) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. So line L4: (4,4)-(6,1) slope -1.5. It also passes through (5,? ) at x=5: y = 4 + (-1.5)*(5-4) = 4 - 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L4 covers A4 and C1.\n\nNow we have covered C1 (6,1). Still uncovered: C2=(6,2) and D1=(7,1). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust assignments to include C2 and D1.\n\nPerhaps we can assign C2 to L1 or L2 or L3 if slopes match.\n\nCheck L1: passes through (4,1) and (5,2) slope 1. At x=6, y = 1 + 1*(6-4) = 3, not 2. So L1 doe