)-(3,4) and (4,1)-(4,4). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover column 5 points: (5,1),(5,2) remain (since (5,3) covered by L1). Column 6 points: (6,1),(6,2). Column 7 point: (7,1).\n\nWe have 4 lines L1-L4, each can have at most one point per column. Let's see which lines have capacity for column 5:\n\n- L1 already has (5,3) (col5). So cannot have another col5 point.\n- L2 has no col5 point yet.\n- L3 has no col5 point yet.\n- L4 has no col5 point yet.\n\nThus we need to assign (5,1) and (5,2) to two of L2, L3, L4.\n\nSimilarly, column 6 points (6,1),(6,2) need to be assigned to two lines among L2, L3, L4 (since L1 already has col6? Actually L1 does not have col6 point yet, but we could assign to L1 as well, but L1 already has col5 point (5,3). It can still have a col6 point because each line can have at most one point per column, but can have points from multiple columns. So L1 can also have a col6 point. However, we need to ensure collinearity: the line L1 is defined by (3,1) and (4,2) (slope 1). If we add a point (6,3) it would be collinear, but (6,3) not in T_n. So L1 cannot include any other point from column 6 because the line y = x-2 at x=6 gives y=4, not 1 or 2. So L1 cannot cover any column 6 point.\n\nThus column 6 points must be assigned to lines L2, L3, L4 (or maybe L1 if we change L1). Let's see.\n\nNow we need to assign (5,1),(5,2) to lines L2, L3, L4 such that each line remains collinear.\n\nCheck line L2: passes through (3,2) and (4,3) slope 1, equation y = x - 1. At x=5, y = 4, not 1 or 2. So L2 cannot cover any column 5 point.\n\nLine L3: passes through (3,3) and (4,4) slope 1, equation y = x. At x=5, y = 5, not 1 or 2. So L3 cannot cover column 5 points.\n\nLine L4: passes through (3,4) and (4,1) slope -3, equation y = -3x + 13. At x=5, y = -15 + 13 = -2, not 1 or 2. So L4 cannot cover column 5 points.\n\nThus none of L2-L4 can cover (5,1) or (5,2). So we need to adjust the lines.\n\nThus the assignment we made for columns 3 and 4 may not allow covering column 5 points.\n\nWe need to find a different set of lines that can also include column 5 points.\n\nPerhaps we can use lines of slope 1 for columns 3-5 as we did for L1, but we need to adjust to include (5,1) and (5,2). Let's try to find lines that pass through (3,1) and (5,1) maybe? That's vertical line x=3? Actually (3,1) and (5,1) have same y coordinate, horizontal line y=1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nLine through (3,1) and (5,2): slope = (2-1)/(5-3) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. Equation: y-1 = 0.5*(x-3) => y = 0.5x - 0.5. At x=4, y = 2 - 0.5 = 1.5, not integer. So line passes through (3,1) and (5,2) only.\n\nThus we could assign (3,1) and (5,2) to a line.\n\nSimilarly, line through (3,2) and (5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Equation: y-2 = -0.5*(x-3) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=4, y = -2 + 3.5 = 1.5, not integer. So line passes through (3,2) and (5,1).\n\nThus we can pair column 3 points with column 5 points.\n\nNow we need to also cover column 4 points.\n\nLet's try to design 4 lines as follows:\n\n- L1: (3,1) and (5,2) (slope 0.5). Also maybe include (4,? )? At x=4, y = 0.5*4 - 0.5 = 2 - 0.5 = 1.5, not integer. So L1 covers only (3,1) and (5,2).\n\n- L2: (3,2) and (5,1) (slope -0.5). At x=4, y = -0.5*4 + 3.5 = -2 + 3.5 = 1.5, not integer. So L2 covers (3,2),(5,1).\n\n- L3: (3,3) and (4,4) maybe? That's slope 1, line y = x. At x=5, y=5 not in T_n. So L3 covers (3,3),(4,4). Could also include (6,5) not in T_n.\n\n- L4: (3,4) and (4,1) slope = (1-4)/(4-3) = -3, line y = -3x + 13, as before. At x=5, y = -2, not in T_n.\n\nNow we have covered column 3 points (3,1)-(3,4). Column 4 points: (4,1) covered by L4, (4,2) not covered, (4,3) not covered, (4,4) covered by L3. Column 5 points: (5,1) covered by L2, (5,2) covered by L1, (5,3) not covered. Column 6 points: (6,1),(6,2) not covered. Column 7 point (7,1) not covered.\n\nWe still have many points uncovered.\n\nThus this approach is not working.\n\nThus constructing a covering for k=4 seems challenging.\n\nBut we need to determine if it's impossible for all n≥3. The solution's argument is flawed, but maybe the result is still correct.\n\nWe need to verify if there exists any n where k=4 is possible. If we can find a counterexample, the answer {0,1,3} would be wrong.\n\nLet's try to search for small n manually for k=4.\n\nn=3: need 3 lines total, k=4 impossible because k cannot exceed n. So k=4 not possible for n=3.\n\nn=4: need 4 lines total, k=4 means all lines sunny. We attempted to find a covering for n=4 with all sunny lines and found it impossible because column 3 has 2 points requiring at least 2 sunny lines, column 4 has 1 point requiring a third line, column 2 has 3 points requiring at least 3 lines, column 1 has 4 points requiring at least 4 lines. So we need at least 4 lines, which we have. But we need to assign points to lines such that each line is collinear. Let's try to see if there is a covering for n=4 with all sunny lines.\n\nWe need to cover points:\n\n- Column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\n- Column 3: (3,1),(3,2)\n- Column 4: (4,1)\n\nWe have 4 lines, all sunny.\n\nEach line can intersect each column at most once. So each line can have at most one point from each column.\n\nThus we need to assign each column's points to distinct lines.\n\nColumn 1 has 4 points, so each line must have exactly one column 1 point.\n\nColumn 2 has 3 points, so three lines will have a column 2 point, one line will not.\n\nColumn 3 has 2 points, so two lines will have a column 3 point.\n\nColumn 4 has 1 point, so one line will have a column 4 point.\n\nThus each line will have at least one point (its column 1 point). Some lines will have additional points.\n\nNow we need to find 4 lines L_i such that:\n\n- L_i passes through (1,a_i) for a_i = 1,2,3,4 (distinct).\n- For three of the lines, also passes through a distinct point in column 2: (2,b_j) for b_j = 1,2,3.\n- For two of the lines, also passes through a distinct point in column 3: (3,c_k) for c_k = 1,2.\n- For one line, also passes through (4,1).\n\nThus we need to assign points to lines such that each line's points are collinear.\n\nWe can try to find such lines.\n\nLet’s denote the lines anchored at (1,a) for a=1,2,3,4.\n\nWe need to assign column 2 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) to three of these lines.\n\nCompute slopes from each anchor to each column 2 point:\n\nAnchor (1,1):\n- to (2,1): slope = 0 (horizontal) not allowed.\n- to (2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, allowed.\n- to (2,3): slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed.\n\nAnchor (1,2):\n- to (2,1): slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- to (2,2): slope = 0, not allowed.\n- to (2,3): slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed.\n\nAnchor (1,3):\n- to (2,1): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed.\n- to (2,2): slope = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- to (2,3): slope = 0, not allowed.\n\nAnchor (1,4):\n- to (2,1): slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed.\n- to (2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed.\n- to (2,3): slope = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus possible assignments (anchor -> column2 point) with allowed slopes:\n\n- (1,1) -> (2,2) (slope 1) or (2,3) (slope 2)\n- (1,2) -> (2,3) (slope 1)\n- (1,3) -> (2,1) (slope -2)\n- (1,4) -> (2,1) (slope -3) or (2,2) (slope -2)\n\nWe need to assign each column 2 point to a distinct anchor.\n\nColumn 2 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Let's try to assign:\n\n- (2,1) to anchor (1,3) (slope -2) or (1,4) (slope -3). Choose (1,3) maybe.\n- (2,2) to anchor (1,1) (slope 1) or (1,4) (slope -2). Choose (1,1) maybe.\n- (2,3) to anchor (1,2) (slope 1) or (1,1) (slope 2). Since (1,1) already used for (2,2), we assign (2,3) to (1,2). So assignment:\n\n- Anchor (1,1) -> (2,2) (slope 1)\n- Anchor (1,2) -> (2,3) (slope 1)\n- Anchor (1,3) -> (2,1) (slope -2)\n- Anchor (1,4) -> no column 2 point.\n\nNow we need to assign column 3 points (3,1),(3,2) to two of the lines.\n\nCompute slopes from each anchor to column 3 points:\n\nAnchor (1,1):\n- to (3,1): slope = (1-1)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- to (3,2