(5,1) to L2 maybe? L2 passes through (3,2) and (4,3) slope 1. At x=5, y = 2 + 1*(5-3) = 4, not 1.\n\nL3 passes through (3,3) and (5,2) slope -0.5. At x=6, y = 3 + (-0.5)*(6-3) = 3 - 1.5 = 1.5, not 1.\n\nL4 passes through (3,4) and (4,1) slope -3. At x=5, y = 4 + (-3)*(5-3) = 4 - 6 = -2, not 1.\n\nThus none cover (5,1) or (6,1). So we need to adjust the assignment.\n\nPerhaps we can use a line that covers (5,1) and (6,1) together? That's horizontal, not allowed. So we need separate lines for (5,1) and (6,1). But we have only 4 lines, all already used for column 3 points. So we need to reassign some column 3 points to lines that also cover (5,1) or (6,1). Let's try to find a line that passes through a column 3 point and (5,1) with a sunny slope.\n\nCheck line through (3,2) and (5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. So line L: (3,2)-(5,1). At x=4, y = 2 + (-0.5)*(4-3) = 2 - 0.5 = 1.5, not integer. So line covers (3,2),(5,1). Good.\n\nNow we can assign (3,2) to this line, which also covers (5,1). Then we need to cover (4,3) maybe with a different line.\n\nSimilarly, line through (3,3) and (6,1): slope = (1-3)/(6-3) = -2/3 ≈ -0.666, sunny. So line passes through (3,3),(6,1). At x=4, y = 3 + (-2/3)*(4-3) = 3 - 2/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. At x=5, y = 3 + (-2/3)*(5-3) = 3 - 4/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667. So line covers (3,3),(6,1). Good.\n\nNow we have lines covering (3,2)-(5,1) and (3,3)-(6,1). That covers column 5 point (5,1) and column 6 point (6,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover remaining points: column 3 points (3,1),(3,4) left; column 4 points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4?) Actually column 4 has points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3). (4,4) sum=8 >7, not in T_n. So column 4 points: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3). Column 5 point (5,2) remains; column 5 point (5,3) not in T_n; column 5 point (5,2) is in T_n. Column 5 point (5,2) remains. Column 5 point (5,2) is not covered yet. Column 5 point (5,2) is in column 5. Column 5 point (5,2) is not covered. Column 5 point (5,2) we need to cover.\n\nNow we have used 2 sunny lines (L_a: (3,2)-(5,1), L_b: (3,3)-(6,1)). We have 2 remaining sunny lines to cover the rest.\n\nRemaining points: (3,1),(3,4) (col3); (4,1),(4,2),(4,3) (col4); (5,2) (col5). That's 6 points left, 2 lines left, each can cover at most one point per column, so across columns 3,4,5 (3 columns), each line can cover at most 3 points, total capacity = 6, which matches the number of points. So we need each line to cover exactly one point from each column.\n\nThus we need to find two lines each passing through one point from column 3, one from column 4, and one from column 5.\n\nLet's try to find such lines.\n\nWe need to pair a column 3 point with a column 4 point and a column 5 point.\n\nPotential combos:\n\n- Choose column 3 point (3,1). Pair with column 4 point (4,2) maybe? Slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1. Then line through (3,1) and (4,2) slope 1. At x=5, y = 1 + 1*(5-3) = 3. So line would pass through (5,3), not (5,2). So not good.\n\n- Pair (3,1) with (4,3): slope = (3-1)/(4-3) = 2, line y-1 = 2*(x-3) => y = 2x -5. At x=5, y = 10-5 =5, not (5,2). So not.\n\n- Pair (3,1) with (4,1): slope = (1-1)/(4-3) = 0, not allowed.\n\n- Pair (3,1) with (4,2) gave slope 1, not covering (5,2). So (3,1) cannot be paired with (5,2) directly because slope = (2-1)/(5-3) = 1/2 = 0.5, line through (3,1) and (5,2) slope 0.5. At x=4, y = 1 + 0.5*(4-3) = 1.5, not integer. So line would not pass through any integer point in column 4. So cannot have a line covering (3,1),(5,2) and also a column 4 point.\n\nThus (3,1) cannot be part of a line that also covers a column 5 point (5,2) while also covering a column 4 point with integer coordinates.\n\nNow try column 3 point (3,4). Pair with column 5 point (5,2): slope = (2-4)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nPair (3,4) with column 5 point (5,2) not allowed.\n\nPair (3,4) with column 5 point (5,2) slope -1, not allowed. So (3,4) cannot be paired with (5,2) either.\n\nThus we cannot have a line that includes a column 5 point (5,2) and a column 3 point (3,1) or (3,4) while also having a column 4 point. So maybe we need to assign (5,2) to a line that does not include a column 3 point, but then that line would have only column 4 and column 5 points, leaving column 3 points uncovered.\n\nBut we have only 2 lines left, and we need to cover 3 column 3 points? Actually we have 2 column 3 points left: (3,1) and (3,4). So each line can cover one of them. So each line must include a column 3 point. So each line must also include a column 5 point (since we have 2 column 5 points left: (5,2) and maybe (5,? ) Actually we have only (5,2) left, because (5,1) covered earlier. So we have only one column 5 point left, but we have 2 lines left. So one line will not have a column 5 point. That's okay.\n\nThus we need to assign (5,2) to one of the two lines, and the other line will not have a column 5 point.\n\nNow we need to assign column 4 points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3) to the two lines, each line can have at most one column 4 point. So we need to assign two of the three column 4 points to the two lines, leaving one column 4 point uncovered. But we have no more lines left. So we need to cover the remaining column 4 point with a non-sunny line, but we have used both non-sunny lines (vertical x=1 and x=2). So cannot.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nThus covering for n=6 with k=4 seems impossible.\n\nThus the conclusion that k≥4 is impossible for all n≥3 seems plausible.\n\nThus answer {0,1,3} is correct.\n\nNow we need to check the solution's reasoning for correctness and validity.\n\nThe solution's reasoning includes:\n\n- Preliminaries: counting points, column sizes.\n\n- Points on a sunny line: they derive that a sunny line can contain at most n+1 points, and more importantly, it can meet each column in at most one point. This is correct: a sunny line cannot be vertical, so at most one point per column.\n\n- Upper bound for number of sunny lines: they argue that for any column, at most k points can be covered 