lumn 4. So each line can have up to 3 points (one from each column). Since we have 6 points, we need each line to have exactly 2 points (since 3 lines * 2 = 6). So each line will have exactly two points, from two different columns. So we need to pair points from different columns.\n\nWe need to pair each point in column 2 with a point in column 3 or 4, such that each point in column 3 and 4 is paired exactly once. Since column 4 has only one point, it must be paired with one of the column 2 points. So one line will have (4,1) and some (2,b). The remaining column 2 points (two left) must be paired with the two column 3 points. So we need lines: L1: (4,1) with (2,b1); L2: (3,1) with (2,b2); L3: (3,2) with (2,b3). So we need to choose b1,b2,b3 distinct among {1,2,3}.\n\nNow we need to check if each pair can be connected by a sunny line (i.e., slope not 0, ∞, -1). Let's examine possible pairs:\n\n- Pair (4,1) with (2,1): slope (1-1)/(4-2) = 0/2 = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So cannot pair (4,1) with (2,1).\n\n- Pair (4,1) with (2,2): slope (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -1/2, allowed (sunny). So L1 could be line through (4,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- Pair (4,1) with (2,3): slope (3-1)/(2-4) = 2/(-2) = -1, not allowed. So cannot pair (4,1) with (2,3).\n\nThus the only viable pairing for (4,1) is with (2,2). So we set b1=2.\n\nNow we have remaining column 2 points: (2,1) and (2,3). They need to be paired with (3,1) and (3,2). Let's check possible pairings:\n\n- Pair (2,1) with (3,1): slope (1-1)/(3-2) = 0, not allowed.\n\n- Pair (2,1) with (3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed (sunny). So (2,1) can pair with (3,2).\n\n- Pair (2,3) with (3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed (sunny). So (2,3) can pair with (3,1).\n\n- Pair (2,3) with (3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we can pair (2,1) with (3,2) and (2,3) with (3,1). So we have three sunny lines:\n\nL1: (4,1)-(2,2) slope -1/2 (sunny)\nL2: (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1 (sunny)\nL3: (2,3)-(3,1) slope -2 (sunny)\n\nThus we have 