r of points in columns v+1,...,n is sum_{i=v+1}^{n} (n+1-i) = sum_{j=1}^{n-v} j = (n - v)(n - v + 1)/2. So we need (n - v)(n - v + 1)/2 ≤ (n - v)*(n - v) => dividing by (n - v) (assuming n - v > 0), we get (n - v + 1)/2 ≤ (n - v) => n - v + 1 ≤ 2(n - v) => n - v + 1 ≤ 2n - 2v => bring terms: 1 ≤ n - v => n - v ≥ 1 => v ≤ n - 1. That's always true for v ≤ n - 1. So the inequality holds for any v ≤ n - 1. So the capacity is enough. So the condition is not restrictive.\n\nThus we can have any number of vertical lines v from 0 to n, and we can cover the rest with n - v other lines, as long as we can assign points appropriately. But we also have constraints on the type of other lines: they can be sunny, horizontal, or diagonal. Horizontal lines are also non-sunny, but they are limited: each horizontal line can intersect each column at most once, but also each horizontal line can intersect each row at most once. But we can treat them similarly to sunny lines: they can cover at most one point per column. So the capacity argument holds for any non-vertical line, regardless of being sunny or not.\n\nThus the only limitation on k is the number of sunny lines we can have among the n - v non-vertical lines. Since we can have at most n - v non-vertical lines, the maximum possible k is n - v (if all non-vertical lines are sunny). So the maximum possible k is n - v, where v is the number of vertical lines. Since v can be as low as 0, we could have up to n sunny lines? But we need to check if we can have all lines sunny (k=n). That would mean we have no vertical lines, all lines are sunny. Then each column i must be covered by at most one point per line, so each column i with height n+1-i must have at most n points (since we have n lines). That's okay because height ≤ n for all columns (since max height is n). So in principle, we could have all lines sunny. But we need to ensure that we can cover all points with n sunny lines. Is that possible? Let's try to construct for n=3: 3 sun