ill be non-sunny. Let's verify: The three points are (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). The slopes between each pair:\n\n- (n-1,1)-(n-1,2): vertical (∞) not sunny.\n- (n-1,1)-(n,1): horizontal (0) not sunny.\n- (n-1,2)-(n,1): slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1, not sunny.\n\nThus any line covering any two of these points is non-sunny. So to cover all three points with two sunny lines, each sunny line can cover at most one of these points. So we would need at least three lines (two sunny + one non-sunny) to cover them. But we have only two sunny lines and we can also use non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/diag) as part of the n lines. So we could have n-2 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2, plus one non-sunny line (maybe horizontal y=1) covering (n-1,1) and (n,1), and then one sunny line covering (n-1,2). That would be k=1, not k=2. Actually we need exactly 2 sunny lines, so we could have n-2 vertical lines, plus one non-sunny line (horizontal y=1) covering (n-1,1) and (n,1), plus two sunny lines covering (n-1,2) and maybe some other points. But that would be n-2 + 1 + 2 = n+1 lines, too many. So we need to drop one vertical line elsewhere to keep total n lines. So we could drop vertical line for column n-2, and use a sunny line to cover some points there. Let's try to design a scheme:\n\n- Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (that's n-3 lines).\n- Use horizontal line y=1 to cover (n-2,1), (n-1,1), (n,1). That's non-sunny.\n- Use sunny line L1 to cover (n-2,2) and maybe other points.\n- Use sunny line L2 to cover (n-2,3) and (n-1,2) maybe.\n- Use vertical line for column n-2? Actually we used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3, not for column n-2. So we need to cover points in column n-2: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Horizontal line y=1 covers (n-2,1). Sunny line L1 could cover (n-2,2). Sunny line L2 could cover (n-2,3). So all points covered. Let's check if L1 and L2 can be sunny lines covering those points and maybe also other points in earlier columns, but that's okay.\n\n